    feat(board): refuse board shrink on the commit path (AUG-194)
    
    Commit 45a29b9 (a lane close-out) rewrote .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl
    from 297 rows to 2 and the commit path waved it through: boardctl validate
    grades a short board as VALID, so only a row-count comparison against HEAD
    can see row loss.
    
    - scripts/board_shrink_check.py: stdlib checker comparing the staged board
      against HEAD:<board>; refuses a shrink, names both counts and the lost
      ids, honours BOARD_ALLOW_SHRINK=1 as a loud override, fails closed (naming
      --deepen) when the baseline blob is unreadable. Header states the honest
      limit: a same-count wholesale replacement is not detected.
    - scripts/install_board_guard.py: installs the checker as a marked managed
      block in .git/hooks/pre-commit, mirroring the boardctl block convention.
    - .github/workflows/ci.yml: --recent check on the checked-out commit plus a
      pytest step for the new suite.
    - tests/test_board_shrink_check.py: 19 tests, incl. the 45a29b9 replay.
    - tests/gate.sh: pytest arm covers the new suite.
    
    AC4: the writer behind 45a29b9 lives outside this repo, so the deliverable
    is the quarantine (the rewrite can no longer land without the override),
    documented in the checker header and proven by the replay test.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
